# Copy to `.env.local`. This file is for your *choices* — which transport a
# fresh browser starts on, whether direct mode is on at all. It is not where
# tokens live, and you should never paste one into it.
#
# `make setup` issues both keys and writes them to `.env.development.local`,
# which Next loads ahead of `.env.local`. So the two token variables below are
# already set by the time this file is read, and setting them here does
# nothing — the generated value wins. That is deliberate: `make setup` drops
# the old keys as it issues new ones, so a token pasted here is stale from the
# next run onward, and a stale token does not announce itself. It just makes
# every request 401.
#
# To reissue by hand — each token prints exactly once, and only an argon2id
# hash is stored, so a lost token is reissued and never recovered:
#
#   make api_key                                          # web key
#   make api_key KEY_SOURCE=payments-api KEY_TYPE=backend  # backend key
#
# Those print to your terminal rather than writing the generated file. Run
# `make setup` to put a fresh pair back in place.

# Where the server-side SDKs post: the Node SDK and the relay route.
POLARIS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4000/v1/events

# Where the browser posts in `direct` mode. Separate from the line above
# because in direct mode this one is reached from the tab, not the server —
# on a real deployment they are usually different hosts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POLARIS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4000/v1/events

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser events. Two transports; they differ in exactly one thing, whether
# the line below is commented out.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `direct` mode: the key is inlined into the JS bundle and sent from the tab.
# That is correct for a web key — the ingester scopes it to the origin
# allow-list and rate limits it per key — and never correct for a backend key.
#
# This is the one token variable you set yourself, because setting it puts a
# key in the JS bundle, and deciding that is the point of this blueprint.
# Uncomment to opt in. Leave it commented to run relay-only: direct mode then
# reports itself unavailable instead of pretending.
#
# Note the `$` — this *references* the issued token rather than copying it, so
# it survives every reissue. Expansion happens across env files, so the value
# comes from `.env.development.local`. Before `make setup` has ever run it
# expands to empty, which is exactly the relay-only case.
#NEXT_PUBLIC_POLARIS_API_KEY=$POLARIS_WEB_API_KEY

# Which transport a fresh browser starts on: `direct` or `relay`. The switch
# on /transport overrides it per browser and remembers the choice.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POLARIS_TRANSPORT=direct

# A label the ingester overwrites with the identifier bound to the key. Kept
# honest anyway — it shows up in logs before the key is resolved.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POLARIS_SOURCE_ID=storefront-web

# `relay` mode needs no browser key at all: `app/api/polaris/events/route.ts`
# reads POLARIS_WEB_API_KEY server-side, from the generated file, and attaches
# it there. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so it never reaches the browser.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend events. Server-side only, always — a backend key is not
# origin-scoped and not rate-limited the way a web key is. The token comes
# from the generated file; only the source label is yours.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

POLARIS_BACKEND_SOURCE_ID=payments-api
